1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,320
In front of you, there's a coin

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:05,280
lying on the ground.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:08,860
Above you, there's the moon.

4
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:11,360
Where will you look?

5
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:12,920
If you're out for an evening walk,

6
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:15,240
you might look up.

7
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:17,500
If you have just enough money for food,

8
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,200
you might pick up the coin.

9
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,320
And if, for years, you've looked at the ground

10
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,870
because you were taught that it was the only

11
00:00:25,870 --> 00:00:29,040
serious thing to do, do you still remember

12
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:31,820
what you were looking for up there?

13
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:34,080
This question makes me think of Walden,

14
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:37,040
the book in which Henry David Thoreau describes

15
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:40,160
his life in a small cabin near a pond.

16
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,000
The same story can provoke

17
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,460
two very different reactions.

18
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:46,790
Someone listens and says that living

19
00:00:46,790 --> 00:00:49,300
alone by the water would be a dream.

20
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:51,290
Time at last to read,

21
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:54,690
to walk and to hear your own thoughts.

22
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:57,680
Someone else asks a very simple question.

23
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:59,190
If I do that,

24
00:00:59,190 --> 00:01:02,100
who will look after the people who rely on me?

25
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:03,840
One looks at the time that this

26
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:05,820
decision could give back to them.

27
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:07,460
The other thinks of the people

28
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:10,460
they already share that time with.

29
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:12,820
They were told the same story.

30
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:16,020
They heard two different questions.

31
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:18,420
Hello and welcome to Cozy French Books.

32
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:19,590
I'm Léa.

33
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:21,970
Here, we discover books together

34
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:24,780
and practise French at our own pace.

35
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:26,220
Along the story,

36
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:28,170
you'll hear words and expressions

37
00:01:28,170 --> 00:01:30,740
that you can use in everyday life.

38
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:33,360
If you like learning French this way,

39
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:36,240
subscribe so you don't miss the next episodes.

40
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:39,860
Today, we're talking about The Moon and Sixpence,

41
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:43,300
a novel by William Somerset Maugham, known

42
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:46,550
in French under the title L'Envoûté.

43
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,920
The English title brings together the moon

44
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:53,340
and a sixpence, a small coin.

45
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:55,470
For our reading, the moon

46
00:01:55,470 --> 00:01:58,200
will be the ideal we want to reach.

47
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:01,470
The coin will be what allows us to live,

48
00:02:01,470 --> 00:02:03,660
to pay for the things we

49
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:06,060
need and to keep our commitments.

50
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:08,100
I'd like us to start from this idea.

51
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:11,420
How you see life changes what you pursue,

52
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:13,820
what you put first and the way

53
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:15,850
you treat other people.

54
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:17,870
What I love about this novel is that it

55
00:02:17,870 --> 00:02:20,420
gives us several people to look at.

56
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:22,860
Turn towards the man who looks up at

57
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:26,140
the moon and you'll see a story of freedom.

58
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:28,130
Turn towards the people who have to

59
00:02:28,130 --> 00:02:30,460
deal with what he leaves behind

60
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:33,380
and you'll see the price of that freedom.

61
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:34,430
In between,

62
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:37,340
there's also someone who chooses to help.

63
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:38,940
It's through these three

64
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:41,390
perspectives that the novel interests me.

65
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:43,940
The way you see success often decides

66
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:47,260
the life you try to build.

67
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:50,070
Charles Strickland, the main character,

68
00:02:50,070 --> 00:02:51,770
could serve as an example

69
00:02:51,770 --> 00:02:54,420
in a conversation about success.

70
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:57,220
He works in London, in finance.

71
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:00,060
He earns enough to support his family.

72
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:01,570
He has a wife,

73
00:03:01,570 --> 00:03:05,060
two children and a life that seems well settled.

74
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,930
If you asked his loved ones what he

75
00:03:06,930 --> 00:03:09,470
should do over the next ten years,

76
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:10,960
many would no doubt say

77
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:12,980
that he should carry on like this.

78
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:15,240
Why change a life in which

79
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:16,860
so many things are going well?

80
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:19,580
One day, when he's about forty,

81
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:21,560
he leaves a short letter

82
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,580
for his wife and goes to Paris.

83
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:25,130
The people who know him

84
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:27,500
immediately look for an explanation.

85
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,350
When a man leaves his family

86
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:31,420
and goes alone to another city,

87
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:34,510
people expect he's left for someone.

88
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,760
The narrator, who goes to see him in Paris,

89
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:38,960
finds a man living on very little

90
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:40,860
money who wants to paint.

91
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:42,980
There's something almost comical

92
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:45,060
in the gap between the explanation

93
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:46,980
everyone expects and the one

94
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:49,340
Strickland ends up giving.

95
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:52,740
In short, they ask him where the woman is.

96
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:55,740
He's already thinking about his painting.

97
00:03:55,740 --> 00:03:58,410
Now look at the two criticisms one

98
00:03:58,410 --> 00:04:01,030
could make of him about this choice.

99
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:03,670
The first is that he gives up a comfortable

100
00:04:03,670 --> 00:04:06,420
life to live on very little.

101
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:09,600
The second is that he starts late and has

102
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:13,060
no guarantee of becoming a good painter.

103
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:15,460
Both remarks are reasonable.

104
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:17,900
If you gave him career advice,

105
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:19,650
you'd ask him how long

106
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:21,860
he can live like that.

107
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:23,930
You'd probably advise him

108
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:26,020
to keep his job and to paint

109
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:28,390
on the side to see how it goes.

110
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,660
For someone who mainly wants to protect

111
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:32,790
a life already built, Strickland's

112
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:35,940
departure is hard to understand.

113
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:37,640
He looks at the situation

114
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:39,940
from another question.

115
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,120
Can he still live his life

116
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:44,340
without making room for painting?

117
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:47,540
Maugham gives him a very simple image.

118
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:50,290
When a person falls into the water, they try

119
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:53,540
to stay afloat with whatever means they have.

120
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:55,240
The question of whether

121
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:57,630
they swim well comes later.

122
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:59,530
For Strickland, painting has

123
00:04:59,530 --> 00:05:01,740
something of that urgency.

124
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:03,730
He feels he has to start,

125
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:07,340
long before knowing if he has the talent.

126
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:09,200
This image doesn't yet say

127
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:11,140
what he owes his family.

128
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:13,300
It explains why the arguments

129
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:15,540
about his age and his chances of success

130
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:17,740
aren't enough to make him come home.

131
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:21,140
So I'm asking you the question.

132
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:24,120
What condition do you think you must meet

133
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:26,830
before doing something that matters?

134
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:28,740
I have to be younger.

135
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:30,740
I have to be good already.

136
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:32,730
Someone has to tell me

137
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:34,740
I'm right to try.

138
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:36,870
You can spend a lot of time

139
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:38,740
waiting for that final sign.

140
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:41,210
And there are things on which others

141
00:05:41,210 --> 00:05:43,740
can give you a very precise opinion.

142
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:45,810
They can talk to you about the cost,

143
00:05:45,810 --> 00:05:47,740
the time or the difficulties.

144
00:05:47,740 --> 00:05:50,740
The place this desire holds in your life,

145
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:53,810
that's mostly something only you can know.

146
00:05:53,860 --> 00:05:55,540
There's a detail that makes Strickland

147
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,340
harder to understand.

148
00:05:57,340 --> 00:05:59,590
He seems uninterested in the signs

149
00:05:59,590 --> 00:06:02,140
of success we attach to an artist.

150
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:04,130
He lives on the bare minimum and puts

151
00:06:04,130 --> 00:06:06,940
his work before his comfort.

152
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:10,140
If he earns a little, he can paint again.

153
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:12,630
He's ready to spend a long time in a room

154
00:06:12,630 --> 00:06:14,580
with his colours, while the people

155
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:16,150
who once gave him a good place

156
00:06:16,150 --> 00:06:19,630
in society now find him ridiculous.

157
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:22,720
By the logic of those around him,

158
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:23,940
he has lost everything.

159
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:25,760
If you look at what he wanted

160
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:27,960
to give his days to, you see why

161
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:30,340
he thinks he's gained something.

162
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:33,140
That brings us back to the two objects of the title.

163
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:34,960
The moon is easy to look at

164
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:36,940
when you already have enough to eat.

165
00:06:36,940 --> 00:06:38,740
And a small coin can take up

166
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:40,770
a big place in your life when

167
00:06:40,770 --> 00:06:43,540
you're one short to pay for dinner.

168
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:46,030
I want to keep this reality in mind,

169
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:48,460
because the novel gets more interesting

170
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:50,540
when both objects matter.

171
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,990
You can value a salary because it

172
00:06:52,990 --> 00:06:55,340
lets you take care of someone.

173
00:06:55,340 --> 00:06:57,330
You can also feel that you've spent so

174
00:06:57,330 --> 00:06:59,940
long looking for a safer income

175
00:06:59,940 --> 00:07:03,340
or a more respected position that you forgot

176
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:06,740
what that security was meant to be for.

177
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:09,000
Both desires can exist

178
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,630
in the same person, on the same day.

179
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,050
When you say a decision is crazy,

180
00:07:14,050 --> 00:07:15,940
ask yourself another question.

181
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:18,540
What am I actually measuring?

182
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:20,140
If I measure income,

183
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:22,740
I hardly understand Strickland's choice.

184
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:25,340
If I measure the time he can spend painting,

185
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:28,540
I understand much better why he did it.

186
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:30,930
As soon as I add the situation of his wife

187
00:07:30,930 --> 00:07:34,540
and children, the question changes again.

188
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:37,030
You can understand the urgency to paint

189
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:39,040
and wonder what a man could

190
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:40,860
have done so that his family,

191
00:07:40,860 --> 00:07:43,940
too, had the chance to decide.

192
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:45,510
And maybe there's already

193
00:07:45,510 --> 00:07:47,740
a small answer for you here.

194
00:07:47,740 --> 00:07:50,120
You can start taking seriously

195
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:52,700
what draws you and also think about

196
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:55,340
how to make room for it.

197
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:57,330
If something "te tient à cœur",

198
00:07:57,330 --> 00:07:59,940
it means it matters a lot to you.

199
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:02,990
To find out, look at what you still

200
00:08:02,990 --> 00:08:06,630
want to do when nobody praises you for it.

201
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:08,610
Would you like to spend part

202
00:08:08,610 --> 00:08:12,140
of your day learning the thing itself?

203
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:14,510
Would you come back to it the next day,

204
00:08:14,510 --> 00:08:17,540
even if you hardly talked about it to anyone?

205
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:19,610
That might be where you can see

206
00:08:19,610 --> 00:08:21,740
which moon you're really thinking of.

207
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:24,210
Our outlook also decides what we put

208
00:08:24,210 --> 00:08:27,540
first when life forces us to choose.

209
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:30,430
To see it, let's go back to London,

210
00:08:30,430 --> 00:08:33,430
to the person who receives the letter.

211
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,790
Strickland's wife thought she knew

212
00:08:35,790 --> 00:08:38,740
the man she'd been living with for years.

213
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:40,820
She discovers he'd travelled alone

214
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:43,740
much of the way in his head.

215
00:08:43,740 --> 00:08:45,730
She has to explain his departure,

216
00:08:45,730 --> 00:08:48,140
support her children and decide

217
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:50,540
how she's going to earn money.

218
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:52,630
All of this begins on the day

219
00:08:52,630 --> 00:08:55,540
her husband leaves her a few lines.

220
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:58,000
One possible reaction would be to say she

221
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,630
cares too much about what people think of her.

222
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:03,190
She's sensitive to her place in society

223
00:09:03,190 --> 00:09:05,340
and to what is said around her.

224
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:08,340
She's ashamed to be left in this way.

225
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:09,850
But if we stop there,

226
00:09:09,850 --> 00:09:12,540
we miss the effort she's making.

227
00:09:12,540 --> 00:09:15,940
The position she had gave her a framework.

228
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:18,320
She knew how to entertain guests

229
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:20,540
and how to run the house.

230
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:23,420
When everything changes, she has to do something

231
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:26,230
her husband didn't think to do for her.

232
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:28,130
She has to ask herself how

233
00:09:28,130 --> 00:09:30,740
to make the following week possible.

234
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:32,740
She learns to type

235
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:35,740
and builds a typing business.

236
00:09:35,740 --> 00:09:37,740
This word seems complicated.

237
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:40,330
It means typing letters or other

238
00:09:40,330 --> 00:09:42,940
texts on a machine to earn a living.

239
00:09:42,940 --> 00:09:44,620
She also changes her way

240
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:47,140
of living to cut her expenses.

241
00:09:47,140 --> 00:09:48,650
She has to learn,

242
00:09:48,650 --> 00:09:51,540
look for work, keep the accounts.

243
00:09:51,540 --> 00:09:54,030
Nothing changes in a single evening.

244
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:56,260
Yet, by dint of hard work,

245
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:58,540
she manages to "reprendre la main".

246
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:00,800
"Reprendre la main", here,

247
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:04,340
means getting back the ability to decide

248
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:06,940
in a situation that had slipped away from you.

249
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:08,550
Look at the two people

250
00:10:08,550 --> 00:10:10,240
at this point in the story.

251
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:11,750
He gives to painting

252
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:14,340
the best hours of his day.

253
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:16,590
She gives hers to the work

254
00:10:16,590 --> 00:10:18,940
that lets her support her family

255
00:10:18,940 --> 00:10:22,020
and regain a place that matters to her.

256
00:10:22,070 --> 00:10:23,170
You might think that one

257
00:10:23,170 --> 00:10:24,940
dreams bigger than the other.

258
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:26,410
You can also look at

259
00:10:26,410 --> 00:10:28,340
what each one is trying to build,

260
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:30,740
and what that construction depends on.

261
00:10:30,740 --> 00:10:33,340
When the novel talks about Strickland's wife,

262
00:10:33,340 --> 00:10:34,940
I see someone who takes pride

263
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:37,650
in being able to stand on her own without

264
00:10:37,650 --> 00:10:40,340
having to wait for her husband to return.

265
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:41,870
For her, it's a project

266
00:10:41,870 --> 00:10:43,940
worth a daily effort.

267
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:45,930
I like that the two paths

268
00:10:45,930 --> 00:10:48,430
ask the same question from two sides.

269
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:50,870
What do you do when what matters

270
00:10:50,870 --> 00:10:53,740
to you takes time, and that time

271
00:10:53,740 --> 00:10:55,740
is already taken up by something else?

272
00:10:55,740 --> 00:10:57,340
We often think we can answer

273
00:10:57,340 --> 00:10:59,540
by saying what we think is important.

274
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:00,310
In real life,

275
00:11:00,310 --> 00:11:03,340
we see the answer in more ordinary moments.

276
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,440
Look at the two hours you have left

277
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:08,140
once the urgent things are done.

278
00:11:08,140 --> 00:11:11,040
Who or what can you give them to?

279
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:13,140
If you give them to someone today,

280
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:16,250
what will have to wait until tomorrow?

281
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:17,880
It's in these moments that a

282
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:19,940
priority takes on a real shape.

283
00:11:19,940 --> 00:11:21,450
Responsibilities can

284
00:11:21,450 --> 00:11:22,740
also be forced upon us.

285
00:11:22,740 --> 00:11:25,140
Strickland's wife has to find work

286
00:11:25,140 --> 00:11:27,340
in conditions she didn't choose.

287
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:29,600
What she does choose is the way

288
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:32,040
she responds to this new situation,

289
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:34,040
with the options she has.

290
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:36,290
When someone says they prefer to keep

291
00:11:36,290 --> 00:11:38,290
a steady income, you can ask them

292
00:11:38,290 --> 00:11:40,740
what that income allows them to protect.

293
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:42,840
You may hear about children,

294
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:45,300
parents or simply the need

295
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:47,440
to find a little peace again.

296
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:49,430
You need to know that answer

297
00:11:49,430 --> 00:11:51,440
to understand their decision.

298
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:53,010
Think also about what the letter

299
00:11:53,010 --> 00:11:54,740
changes for the husband and wife.

300
00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:57,440
When Charles decides to leave, he thinks of all

301
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:00,140
the time he wants to devote to painting.

302
00:12:00,140 --> 00:12:03,540
His wife, on the same day, discovers

303
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:06,540
a mountain of things he leaves her to sort out.

304
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:09,310
What looks like a grand gesture of freedom

305
00:12:09,310 --> 00:12:12,510
for him becomes a series of chores for her.

306
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:15,060
You can respect someone's desire and talk

307
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:17,940
about the very concrete effects of their decision.

308
00:12:17,940 --> 00:12:19,540
That can start with the questions

309
00:12:19,540 --> 00:12:22,140
the novel leaves to its readers.

310
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:24,240
Who will look after the children?

311
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:25,750
How long does it take

312
00:12:25,750 --> 00:12:27,640
for each of them to find an income again?

313
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:29,120
Which decisions should

314
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:30,540
have been made together?

315
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:32,050
There are people who look up

316
00:12:32,050 --> 00:12:34,040
because they need a purpose.

317
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:36,450
There are people who pick up the coin because

318
00:12:36,450 --> 00:12:38,540
their way of loving goes through the things

319
00:12:38,540 --> 00:12:40,930
they take care of every day.

320
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:43,010
And sometimes, you'll do both

321
00:12:43,010 --> 00:12:44,840
in the same week.

322
00:12:44,840 --> 00:12:46,740
The question I keep from this

323
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:48,640
part of the book is very simple.

324
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:50,440
Do I know why I'm giving

325
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:52,640
my time to this thing today?

326
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:54,840
If you know what it's for in your life,

327
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:56,830
you'll be able to explain your choice without

328
00:12:56,830 --> 00:12:59,340
asking everyone else to make the same one.

329
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:00,940
And you'll be able to look at other people's

330
00:13:00,940 --> 00:13:04,140
choices with a little more curiosity.

331
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:06,970
The way you look at life finally decides

332
00:13:06,970 --> 00:13:10,230
how you treat other people.

333
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:11,760
And this is where I'd like

334
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:13,340
to tell you about Dirk Stroeve.

335
00:13:13,340 --> 00:13:16,140
He's a Dutch painter who lives in Paris.

336
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:18,390
In the narrator's eyes, he doesn't have

337
00:13:18,390 --> 00:13:20,340
a great talent for painting.

338
00:13:20,340 --> 00:13:22,740
And yet, when he looks at a painting,

339
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:24,730
he sees things that more self-assured

340
00:13:24,730 --> 00:13:26,640
painters don't notice.

341
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:28,630
Very early on, he recognises the power

342
00:13:28,630 --> 00:13:30,540
of Strickland's work.

343
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:32,090
Others may make fun

344
00:13:32,090 --> 00:13:33,740
of the man or of his canvases.

345
00:13:33,740 --> 00:13:37,020
Dirk trusts what he sees

346
00:13:37,020 --> 00:13:39,020
enough to stay close to him.

347
00:13:39,070 --> 00:13:41,510
A painter can spend his life learning to make

348
00:13:41,510 --> 00:13:44,050
beautiful paintings and struggle to recognise

349
00:13:44,050 --> 00:13:46,740
beauty in someone else's work.

350
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:49,470
Dirk sees that beauty while no one

351
00:13:49,470 --> 00:13:51,740
can yet tell him that he's right.

352
00:13:51,740 --> 00:13:54,740
He also sees a sick man who needs help.

353
00:13:54,740 --> 00:13:57,140
When Strickland falls seriously ill,

354
00:13:57,140 --> 00:14:00,040
Dirk wants to take him in and nurse him.

355
00:14:00,040 --> 00:14:02,540
Blanche, his wife, is against it.

356
00:14:02,540 --> 00:14:04,030
He insists.

357
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:05,510
The way he sees it,

358
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:07,730
leaving someone in that state is

359
00:14:07,730 --> 00:14:10,440
harder than opening the door to him.

360
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:12,540
This choice costs him dearly.

361
00:14:12,540 --> 00:14:14,530
When Strickland gets better,

362
00:14:14,530 --> 00:14:16,640
Blanche leaves Dirk for him.

363
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:18,960
Dirk suffers, then ends up leaving them

364
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:21,140
the studio where he used to work.

365
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:23,950
What strikes me is what he still hopes for

366
00:14:23,950 --> 00:14:27,040
while he himself has just lost his place.

367
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:29,440
He wants Blanche to be happy.

368
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:30,890
Meanwhile,

369
00:14:30,890 --> 00:14:33,270
Strickland mostly thinks about painting.

370
00:14:33,320 --> 00:14:36,420
One agrees to lose something to give

371
00:14:36,420 --> 00:14:38,940
another person what she asks for.

372
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:41,770
The other is quick to accept people drifting away

373
00:14:41,770 --> 00:14:43,940
as soon as he no longer wants them around.

374
00:14:43,940 --> 00:14:47,240
For Blanche, living with Strickland

375
00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:50,140
has a meaning he doesn't share.

376
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:52,630
She seems ready to pay the price of others'

377
00:14:52,630 --> 00:14:55,740
judgement to be with the man she desires.

378
00:14:55,740 --> 00:14:58,060
She expects this love to bring presence,

379
00:14:58,060 --> 00:15:00,130
a life that is shared.

380
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:02,490
For Strickland, work takes up all

381
00:15:02,490 --> 00:15:05,340
the space again and the relationship ends.

382
00:15:05,340 --> 00:15:07,840
Blanche dies shortly after.

383
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:10,940
This part of the book is painful.

384
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:12,740
And when I hear people say the two of them

385
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:15,140
simply didn't have the same priorities,

386
00:15:15,140 --> 00:15:18,840
I want to ask another question.

387
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:20,920
When you know someone expects from you

388
00:15:20,920 --> 00:15:22,940
something you don't want to give them,

389
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:26,330
when do you tell them?

390
00:15:26,380 --> 00:15:28,970
People can want different lives

391
00:15:28,970 --> 00:15:30,540
and talk early enough so that each one

392
00:15:30,540 --> 00:15:33,540
can choose with the same information.

393
00:15:33,540 --> 00:15:35,170
There's still one thing that's hard

394
00:15:35,170 --> 00:15:36,740
to understand about Dirk.

395
00:15:36,740 --> 00:15:38,730
Even after Blanche's death,

396
00:15:38,730 --> 00:15:42,040
he can still see Strickland's talent.

397
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:43,580
He even goes as far as offering

398
00:15:43,580 --> 00:15:45,540
to take him with him to Holland.

399
00:15:45,540 --> 00:15:47,340
Strickland refuses.

400
00:15:47,340 --> 00:15:49,490
The reader may look at this gesture

401
00:15:49,490 --> 00:15:52,130
and think Dirk should protect himself.

402
00:15:52,180 --> 00:15:54,120
Or they may wonder how this

403
00:15:54,120 --> 00:15:56,030
man manages to keep such

404
00:15:56,030 --> 00:15:58,840
generosity after what he's been through.

405
00:15:58,840 --> 00:16:01,340
Both reactions make sense.

406
00:16:01,340 --> 00:16:03,240
If you put yourself in Dirk's place,

407
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:06,340
you have to hold together the pain he feels

408
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:09,140
and the admiration he never lost.

409
00:16:09,140 --> 00:16:10,840
What moves me about him

410
00:16:10,840 --> 00:16:13,110
is that he often gives without expecting

411
00:16:13,110 --> 00:16:15,440
the other to give anything back.

412
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:18,260
And yet, if a friend acted like him,

413
00:16:18,260 --> 00:16:20,050
I'd ask him if he can still

414
00:16:20,050 --> 00:16:21,930
say no.

415
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:24,200
There's a world between offering a room

416
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:26,450
for a few days and letting someone

417
00:16:26,450 --> 00:16:29,940
decide for you what happens in your home.

418
00:16:29,940 --> 00:16:33,240
In both cases, you may want to help.

419
00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:34,680
In both cases,

420
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:37,850
you can also listen to the person you live with

421
00:16:37,850 --> 00:16:41,540
and ask what effect this choice will have on her.

422
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:43,860
It's a way of caring for others

423
00:16:43,860 --> 00:16:46,450
that isn't always seen, because it

424
00:16:46,450 --> 00:16:49,930
sometimes starts with a difficult conversation.

425
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:51,550
I also find that Dirk

426
00:16:51,550 --> 00:16:53,840
makes us rethink what we admire.

427
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:56,940
When a painter is recognised, we remember his name.

428
00:16:56,940 --> 00:16:58,930
We talk about the strength it took him

429
00:16:58,930 --> 00:17:01,540
to keep going and the years during which

430
00:17:01,540 --> 00:17:03,740
nobody understood what he was doing.

431
00:17:03,740 --> 00:17:05,640
Who remembers the person who

432
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:07,740
first recognised his paintings?

433
00:17:07,740 --> 00:17:09,810
Who remembers the one who

434
00:17:09,810 --> 00:17:12,240
gave him a bed when he was ill?

435
00:17:12,240 --> 00:17:13,750
The novel gives a big

436
00:17:13,750 --> 00:17:15,730
place to Strickland's desire.

437
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:17,960
For me, what also stays is the image

438
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:20,140
of Dirk opening his door.

439
00:17:20,140 --> 00:17:22,210
A life can have a lot of value

440
00:17:22,210 --> 00:17:23,950
because of the gestures that allowed

441
00:17:23,950 --> 00:17:26,140
someone else to keep going.

442
00:17:26,140 --> 00:17:28,130
There's a common French expression

443
00:17:28,130 --> 00:17:30,940
that comes to me when I think of this character.

444
00:17:30,940 --> 00:17:32,640
"Rendre service".

445
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:35,240
It means doing something to help someone.

446
00:17:35,240 --> 00:17:37,840
You can help out by lending money,

447
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:39,850
by offering a place to sleep

448
00:17:39,850 --> 00:17:42,290
or simply by taking the time to look at

449
00:17:42,290 --> 00:17:45,060
someone's work with attention.

450
00:17:45,110 --> 00:17:47,360
Dirk does all of this feeling

451
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:49,140
that these gestures matter.

452
00:17:49,140 --> 00:17:51,730
Strickland often seems to take what people

453
00:17:51,730 --> 00:17:54,340
offer him as if the rest went without saying.

454
00:17:54,340 --> 00:17:56,840
Look at the two men through this word

455
00:17:56,840 --> 00:17:59,920
and ask yourself which of the two you'd want

456
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:02,740
by your side on a day when you need help.

457
00:18:02,740 --> 00:18:04,810
The answer might tell you something

458
00:18:04,810 --> 00:18:06,460
other than the name of the one whose

459
00:18:06,460 --> 00:18:08,340
paintings you'd like to see.

460
00:18:08,340 --> 00:18:10,110
And that's also where I find

461
00:18:10,110 --> 00:18:12,530
the opening phrase interesting.

462
00:18:12,580 --> 00:18:14,860
How you see the world changes

463
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:17,540
what you notice in people.

464
00:18:17,540 --> 00:18:20,350
If all that matters to you is finishing your

465
00:18:20,350 --> 00:18:23,390
work, you risk looking at a person

466
00:18:23,390 --> 00:18:26,440
only when they help you or get in your way.

467
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:28,140
If you care

468
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:30,680
about what others go through, you might

469
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:33,440
hear the question they don't dare to ask.

470
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:36,440
This outlook shows in specific actions.

471
00:18:36,440 --> 00:18:38,120
Do you let someone know

472
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:39,830
when your plans change?

473
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:42,940
Do you listen to their answer to the end?

474
00:18:42,940 --> 00:18:45,570
If you help them, do you ask them

475
00:18:45,570 --> 00:18:47,940
what would really be useful to them?

476
00:18:47,940 --> 00:18:50,330
That's where your ideas about life start

477
00:18:50,330 --> 00:18:52,740
to change someone else's life.

478
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:54,250
So how can we live

479
00:18:54,250 --> 00:18:56,740
with as few regrets as possible?

480
00:18:56,740 --> 00:18:59,520
Closing the book, I go back to the person

481
00:18:59,520 --> 00:19:02,350
who saw a promise of freedom in Thoreau's

482
00:19:02,350 --> 00:19:04,930
cabin, then to the one who wondered

483
00:19:04,930 --> 00:19:07,450
who would look after their loved ones.

484
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:09,090
One asks a question you

485
00:19:09,090 --> 00:19:10,940
can avoid for years.

486
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:14,540
What life do I really want to live?

487
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:17,640
The other asks a question you can forget

488
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:20,740
at the moment you finally find an answer.

489
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:24,540
Who will need to know what I want to do?

490
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:26,050
I think we can give ourselves

491
00:19:26,050 --> 00:19:27,740
time to answer both.

492
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:30,060
You don't have to live to get

493
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:33,530
the approval of everyone around you.

494
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:35,510
Some will ask you to keep

495
00:19:35,510 --> 00:19:37,740
a job you'd like to leave.

496
00:19:37,740 --> 00:19:39,730
Others will tell you that you lack

497
00:19:39,730 --> 00:19:42,740
courage because you choose to stay.

498
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:46,940
You know details that they don't see.

499
00:19:46,940 --> 00:19:49,940
Take the time to name what matters to you,

500
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:53,140
then look at what this choice asks of you

501
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:55,740
and of the people who rely on you.

502
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:58,940
Freedom can begin in that conversation.

503
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:01,530
You have the right to say what you want.

504
00:20:01,580 --> 00:20:03,880
Others have the right to tell you

505
00:20:03,880 --> 00:20:06,040
what it changes for them.

506
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:08,620
If you think back to the moon and the coin after

507
00:20:08,620 --> 00:20:11,350
this episode, ask yourself what each one

508
00:20:11,350 --> 00:20:13,740
represents for you today.

509
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:15,730
The moon could be something

510
00:20:15,730 --> 00:20:18,040
you've wanted to learn for a long time.

511
00:20:18,040 --> 00:20:20,380
The coin could be the way

512
00:20:20,380 --> 00:20:23,640
to protect what already makes your life good.

513
00:20:23,640 --> 00:20:24,740
For someone else,

514
00:20:24,740 --> 00:20:26,840
the answers will be different.

515
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:30,140
And yours may change in ten years.

516
00:20:30,190 --> 00:20:31,830
That's one of the reasons why

517
00:20:31,830 --> 00:20:33,240
I'd recommend this novel.

518
00:20:33,240 --> 00:20:35,490
It gives words to a question you can

519
00:20:35,490 --> 00:20:37,740
ask yourself at many moments in a life,

520
00:20:37,740 --> 00:20:40,550
knowing you may never answer it

521
00:20:40,550 --> 00:20:43,240
the same way twice.

522
00:20:43,240 --> 00:20:45,500
If you want to continue the conversation,

523
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:47,640
leave me a sentence in French.

524
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:49,340
You can start with

525
00:20:49,340 --> 00:20:52,340
"Right now, my moon is…"

526
00:20:52,340 --> 00:20:56,520
or with "What I want to protect is…".

527
00:20:56,570 --> 00:20:58,690
And if you thought of someone while listening,

528
00:20:58,690 --> 00:21:00,840
you can share this episode with them

529
00:21:00,840 --> 00:21:03,240
and ask them the same question.

530
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:05,370
Subscribe to Cozy French Books if

531
00:21:05,370 --> 00:21:07,940
you want to read another book with me.

532
00:21:07,940 --> 00:21:10,260
Thank you for spending this time here.

533
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:11,550
I'm Léa.

534
00:21:11,550 --> 00:21:13,240
See you very soon.
